3. INVESTIGATE THE ROOT CAUSES OF STICKINESS

Do not randomly increase movement statistics or shorten every animation.

Identify the exact mechanisms responsible for the feeling of lost control.

Pay particular attention to the following known characteristics of the existing implementation. Confirm them against the latest code.

3.1 Attack movement defaults

In js/game/combat/attacks.js, existing attack defaults have historically included approximately:

lockMovement: true,
momentum: 1,
airMomentum: 1,
control: 0,
airControl: 0,
friction: 1

This means an ordinary attack generally inherits its incoming momentum but does not permit normal directional steering unless the attack explicitly defines it.

The retained velocity may then be reduced by friction during the attack.

This is a major candidate for why attacks feel planted, particularly when the player is actively holding a movement direction.

Examine every character’s explicit attack overrides before changing the defaults.

3.2 Steering coupling

Inspect steer() in js/game/fighters/movement.js.

Its existing implementation uses movement control not only to scale acceleration, but also to scale the velocity toward which the fighter steers:

const top = mv.maxSpeed * control;
const accel = baseAcceleration * control;

This creates a potentially undesirable coupling.

For example, partial attack control may simultaneously give the player weaker acceleration and make the attack’s effective target speed much lower than ordinary running speed.

The result can be a fighter that technically has directional control but still feels heavily slowed during an attack.

Investigate this carefully.

Do not assume that increasing control alone completely solves the problem.

Consider decoupling steering authority from desired movement speed.

3.3 Combat Assist and lost momentum

Inspect Combat Assist, including:

Fighter.tryCombatAssist()

Fighter.stepCombatAssist()

Fighter.finishCombatAssist()

Combat Assist can automatically move the player’s fighter toward an opponent before executing a melee attack.

The current implementation has historically reset velocity at the completion of the approach.

This can produce abrupt stops and a feeling that the game is taking control of the character.

It can also produce unwanted automatic repositioning.

Both should be investigated.

3.4 Dash restrictions

Alva already has explicit ground dashes and air dashes.

Historically, the shared values have included approximately:

* Ground Dash speed: 1250 units/s.
* Ground Dash duration: 1/6 second.
* Air dash speed: 1250 units/s.
* Air dash duration: 1/6 second.
* Dash cancel time: 0.05 seconds.
* One air dash per airtime.
* Dash Energy cost: 25.
* A 0.5-second movement reuse cooldown.

These are not inherently bad numbers.

However, the combined Energy cost, cooldown, steering restrictions, and interaction with other actions may make mobility feel unnecessarily expensive.

Determine whether the restrictions are justified.

3.5 Attack and movement transitions

Alva already supports important cancellation mechanics, including player-requested attack cuts, hit-cancels, dash-cancels, and landing cancels.

Study these systems before modifying them.

In particular, Fighter.playerMayCut() already permits certain new player requests to interrupt an attack.

Do not implement a second competing cancellation system when the existing one can be refined.

Investigate whether the uncomfortable feeling comes from attack movement itself, action prerequisites, input buffers, repeat cooldowns, or transitions between state machines.

3.6 Animation and perceived responsiveness

An attack can be logically responsive while still looking unresponsive.

Inspect how animation playback corresponds to gameplay state.

Check whether apparent stickiness comes from:

* Repeated animation restarts.
* Visually long recovery poses.
* Attack animations continuing after control has returned.
* Delayed movement animation changes.
* Landing poses that appear to hold a fighter in place.
* Abrupt animation switching.
* Movement state and visual state disagreeing.
* Hitstop or attack feedback being disproportionate to the hit.

Distinguish real input restrictions from animation presentation problems.

