14. IMPROVE GAME FEEL WITHOUT OVERUSING VISUAL EFFECTS

Responsiveness includes visual clarity.

Once the mechanical changes are in place, inspect whether the animations and visual feedback make those changes apparent.

14.1 Animation responsiveness

Ensure animation state accurately follows gameplay state.

A fighter that has regained control should visually communicate that control.

Check:

* Idle-to-run transitions.
* Running attacks.
* Jump takeoff.
* Air movement.
* Air attacks.
* Dash transitions.
* Landing recovery.
* Attack cancellation.
* Hit reactions.
* Direction reversals.

Do not require new sprite assets to implement the core improvements.

Use existing animations appropriately.

14.2 Hitstop

Preserve the satisfying impact of attacks.

Light attacks should generally produce shorter hitstop than dramatic heavy hits.

Avoid excessive freeze that repeatedly interrupts the rhythm of basic exchanges.

Do not eliminate hitstop altogether.

Check that buffered inputs and velocity preservation work correctly through impact freeze.

14.3 Movement feedback

If possible with existing assets, polish the sense of acceleration, momentum, dashing, and landing.

Avoid unnecessary camera shake, screen flashes, exaggerated zoom, or particle spam.

Alva’s visual identity should remain coherent and polished.

14.4 Distinguish mechanical responsiveness from animation timing

Before changing an animation’s speed, prove whether the problem is visual timing or real input restriction.

Do not break the alignment between animation frames and active combat frames.

