18. MEASURE RESPONSIVENESS BEFORE AND AFTER

Create a compact development-only methodology to evaluate the changes.

Avoid introducing a large permanent diagnostics framework unless clearly necessary.

Track useful metrics such as:

Metric	Why it matters
Input-to-action latency	Measures when valid commands take effect
Time to reverse direction	Measures movement agility
Run-to-attack velocity retention	Reveals unwanted attack braking
Attack steering displacement	Shows how much positioning control attacks permit
Dash-to-attack continuity	Reveals transition velocity loss
Air attack steering authority	Measures aerial freedom
Landing-to-action latency	Reveals landing friction
Unintended velocity resets	Identifies abrupt state transitions
Percentage of time in involuntary states	Helps characterize combat pacing
Dash availability over time	Reveals whether the movement economy feels overly restrictive

Use consistent, reproducible input sequences.

When evaluating before and after:

* Use the same fighter.
* Use the same stage.
* Use the same starting position.
* Use the same input sequence.
* Use the same simulation timing.

These measurements cannot prove that a game is fun, but they can expose changes in responsiveness and control.

Separate measured results from subjective design judgments.

