20. IMPORTANT ENGINEERING RULES

Follow these strictly.

Rule 1: Do not rewrite working systems unnecessarily.

Rule 2: Use the existing movement architecture wherever practical.

Rule 3: Keep universal baseline locomotion shared by every fighter.

Rule 4: Preserve character-specific movement mechanics.

Rule 5: No new framework or build process.

Rule 6: Preserve deterministic fixed-step gameplay.

Rule 7: Do not replace physics with direct position teleportation.

Rule 8: Do not add hidden invulnerability, unlimited resources, or illegal cancels.

Rule 9: Do not silently change unrelated balance mechanics.

Rule 10: Do not modify every attack with identical values without considering its role.

Rule 11: Do not change animation timing without verifying gameplay synchronization.

Rule 12: Do not break keyboard, controller, or touch support.

Rule 13: Avoid unnecessary allocations or performance-heavy operations in the fighter update loop.

Rule 14: Make tuning values centralized or clearly authored in existing data structures.

Rule 15: Preserve existing player settings where possible.

Rule 16: Add regression tests for every material new mechanic.

Rule 17: Update the specification when gameplay rules intentionally change.

Rule 18: Do not delete tests merely because they detect changed behaviour. Replace obsolete assertions with tests expressing the new intentional behaviour.

Rule 19: Do not claim the game has been playtested if only automated tests were run.

Rule 20: Implement a cohesive, working improvement rather than a collection of disconnected tweaks.

