21. THE TARGET PLAY EXPERIENCE

The finished system should support the following situations naturally.

Scenario A — Fluid ground pressure

A player runs toward an opponent.

They perform a quick attack without coming to an abrupt stop.

While the attack is happening, they continue influencing their horizontal position.

The attack ends or is legally cancelled.

The player can promptly move, follow up, jump, or retreat.

The exchange feels continuous.

Scenario B — Responsive aerial combat

A player jumps toward an opponent.

They perform an aerial attack while drifting forward.

The opponent moves away.

The player adjusts their position as far as the move’s steering rules reasonably permit.

They land and transition smoothly into the next legal action.

The player feels in control throughout the sequence.

Scenario C — Deliberate heavy attack

The player commits to a powerful attack.

The fighter has reduced mobility because the move is intentionally heavy.

The attack looks and feels impactful.

The opponent can punish the commitment if it misses.

This does not feel like input lag: it feels like a meaningful tactical choice.

Scenario D — Dash into combat

The player initiates a dash.

They attack at the earliest legal cancel window.

Momentum transitions smoothly into the attack.

The fighter does not abruptly lose its speed without a deliberate attack-specific reason.

Scenario E — Defensive repositioning

The player defends against an attack.

Once the relevant blockstun or recovery has ended, movement becomes available promptly.

The player does not have to fight awkward input timing to resume control.

Scenario F — Precise platform navigation

The player jumps between platforms.

They adjust their landing position accurately.

They can use ordinary aerial attacks and movement without feeling stuck.

Where supported, they can intentionally drop through eligible one-way platforms.

They never accidentally phase through solid geometry.

Scenario G — Combat Assist optional

A player who wants manual spacing can disable Combat Assist and maintain full control of their attacks’ positioning.

A player who enables Combat Assist receives helpful approach assistance without sudden, unexplained velocity loss.

Both experiences feel coherent.

