24. FINAL DESIGN PRINCIPLE

The single most important principle behind this entire task is:

The player should feel continuous ownership of their fighter.

Attacking should not arbitrarily remove movement.

Jumping should not feel disconnected from combat.

Landing should not unnecessarily stop the flow.

Dashing should feel like a useful part of the movement system rather than a disruptive scripted action.

Aerial combat should provide real positioning freedom.

Heavy attacks can remain committed because commitment creates meaningful tactical decisions.

Forced states such as hitstun and paralysis can restrict the player because their restrictions are part of combat.

But ordinary fighting should never feel restrictive simply because the implementation defaults to disabling control.

The goal is not maximum speed.

The goal is a fighter that responds to the player’s intentions, permits creative movement, preserves the consequences of their decisions, and feels enjoyable even before advanced mechanics are mastered.

A successful overhaul should make Alva noticeably more enjoyable to play within the first 30 seconds of an ordinary fight.

Its controls should feel intuitive immediately, while its full movement and combat systems still offer substantial depth to master.

Prioritize control freedom, animation-to-gameplay consistency, smooth momentum, and reliable transitions over raw speed increases.

